@prefix sagebrain: <https://w3id.org/synapse/sagebrain#> .
@prefix biolink:   <https://w3id.org/biolink/vocab/> .
@prefix shape:     <https://w3id.org/synapse/sagebrain/shapes#> .
@prefix sh:        <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix owl:       <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:      <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:       <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:      <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#################################################################
#    Shapes graph
#################################################################

<https://w3id.org/synapse/sagebrain/shapes>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:title "SageBrain Graph Model -- SHACL shapes" ;
    rdfs:comment """Constraints for the SageBrain graph model. Kept separate from the
ontology so that the ontology stays a monotonic, open-world T-Box for reasoners while
validation lives here under closed-world semantics.

Three groups of shapes:

  1. MODEL INTEGRITY -- validates the ontology itself: that every element class and every
     connection carries its required metadata. Run these over ontology/main/sagebrain.ttl
     plus its import modules to catch transcription errors when the model is revised.

  2. INSTANCE SHAPES -- validates graph data against the schema. This is also the only
     place the exact permitted (source, target) pairings are recorded: the ontology can
     only state the union over each property's uses. Two things here are strictly
     stronger than the ontology's OWL axioms:
       - sh:class is asserted per (source, target) pair, whereas rdfs:range on a reused
         property can only state the union of all its targets. Where a property's domain
         and range are both unions, that union can permit combinations the model does not.
       - cardinality is scoped per source shape, so relates_to can be at most 1 from
         DiseaseStage while remaining unbounded from Pathology.

  3. INVERSE CARDINALITY (DEACTIVATED) -- see the extended note in that section. The
     source model's cardinality column cannot be mechanically compiled in the inverse
     direction, so those shapes are authored but carry sh:deactivated true.""" ;
    rdfs:seeAlso "See the VALIDATION CONFIGURATION section below before running these shapes." ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/synapse/sagebrain> ;
    owl:versionIRI <https://w3id.org/synapse/sagebrain/shapes/0.2.0> ;
    owl:versionInfo "v0.2" ;
    dcterms:created "2026-08-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://github.com/Sage-Bionetworks/sagebrain-model> .


#################################################################
#################################################################
##
##  VALIDATION CONFIGURATION -- READ THIS FIRST
##
##  These shapes MUST be run WITHOUT RDFS or OWL entailment over the data
##  graph. Enabling inference silently disables every sh:class constraint
##  here.
##
##  Why: the ontology declares rdfs:range on its object properties, and
##  rdfs:range is an entailment rule, not a check. Given
##
##      sagebrain:has_diagnosis rdfs:range biolink:Disease .
##      ex:ind1 sagebrain:has_diagnosis ex:pathway1 .
##
##  an RDFS inferencer derives "ex:pathway1 a biolink:Disease",
##  manufacturing exactly the type the shape is testing for. The constraint
##  then passes vacuously no matter what ex:pathway1 actually is. Verified:
##  tests/violating.ttl, with six planted defects, reports 8 violations with
##  inference off and 7 with inference='rdfs', the suppressed one being a range
##  error. (Eight rather than six because two defects each trip both an
##  sh:class and an sh:nodeKind constraint.)
##
##  Correct invocation (pySHACL):
##
##      pyshacl -s ontology/shacl/sagebrain-shapes.ttl \
##             -e <(cat ontology/main/sagebrain.ttl ontology/imports/biolink.ttl) \
##             data.ttl
##
##  Note -e (ont_graph) rather than -i (inference). The ontology is supplied
##  so that class hierarchies resolve; no entailment is computed. The biolink
##  import module goes in with it: the shapes target biolink:Gene and
##  biolink:Pathway, which sagebrain.ttl uses but does not declare. Subclass
##  targeting still works -- sh:targetClass traverses rdfs:subClassOf* by
##  definition, so IndividualShape reaches Case and Control without any
##  inferencing. See tests/validate.py for the exercised configuration.
##
##  This tension between rdfs:range and sh:class is a known consequence of
##  carrying both layers; resolving it properly is tracked as a cleanup item
##  rather than papered over here.
##
#################################################################
#################################################################


#################################################################
#################################################################
##
##  1. MODEL INTEGRITY
##
##  Target: the ontology document itself, not graph data.
##
#################################################################
#################################################################

shape:ConnectionShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf sagebrain:weighted ;
    rdfs:label "Connection shape" ;
    rdfs:comment """Every connection carried over from the source model's Connections sheet must
be documented and must declare whether it is weighted. Targets subjects of sagebrain:weighted
rather than owl:ObjectProperty, so that the shape says "everything the sheet calls a
connection", not "every property that happens to exist".""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path rdfs:label ;
        sh:name "label" ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:datatype xsd:string ;
        sh:message "A connection needs an rdfs:label, as a plain string with no language tag." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:weighted ;
        sh:name "weighted" ;
        sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:datatype xsd:boolean ;
        sh:message "sagebrain:weighted must be exactly one xsd:boolean." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:optional ;
        sh:name "optional" ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:datatype xsd:boolean ;
        sh:message "sagebrain:optional, when present, must be a single xsd:boolean." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path owl:versionInfo ;
        sh:name "version" ;
        sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A connection needs exactly one owl:versionInfo." ;
    ] .

shape:ElementClassShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf sagebrain:elementCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Element class shape" ;
    rdfs:comment "Every element type carried over from the source model's Elements sheet must be documented and categorised." ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path rdfs:label ;
        sh:name "label" ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:datatype xsd:string ;
        sh:message "An element class needs an rdfs:label, as a plain string with no language tag." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:elementCategory ;
        sh:name "element category" ;
        sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:class sagebrain:ElementCategory ;
        sh:message "An element class needs exactly one sagebrain:elementCategory drawn from the category scheme." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path owl:versionInfo ;
        sh:name "version" ;
        sh:minCount 1 ; sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "An element class needs exactly one owl:versionInfo." ;
    ] .

shape:ElementDescriptionShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf sagebrain:elementCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Element description shape" ;
    rdfs:comment """Every element type needs the description the source sheet gives it, but in
one of two places depending on who owns the term. A class minted here carries it as
rdfs:comment, the ordinary place to define your own class. A class reused from another
vocabulary carries it as skos:scopeNote: rdfs:comment on an imported class reads as a
definition of that class, which is not ours to give -- Biolink defines those terms itself,
with skos:definition. Hence sh:or rather than one required path.

Note the looseness: this accepts either path on any element class, because deciding which
one applies means testing the term's namespace, and that is not worth an sh:sparql
constraint here. Which path is correct for a given class is a convention, documented in the
"Reused terms" section of the ontology; what this shape guarantees is that a description
exists and is a plain string.""" ;
    sh:or (
        [ sh:property [ sh:path rdfs:comment ;    sh:minCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ] ]
        [ sh:property [ sh:path skos:scopeNote ; sh:minCount 1 ; sh:datatype xsd:string ] ]
    ) ;
    sh:message "An element class needs its description as a plain string with no language tag -- rdfs:comment if the class is minted here, skos:scopeNote if it is reused from another vocabulary." .


#################################################################
#################################################################
##
##  2. INSTANCE SHAPES
##
##  Target: graph data. One node shape per element class that has at
##  least one outgoing connection. Biodomain and Tissue are omitted --
##  they appear only as targets in the model, so they carry no outgoing
##  constraints. Case and Control are validated by IndividualShape,
##  since SHACL class targets include subclasses.
##
#################################################################
#################################################################

###  Biological  ###############################################

shape:GeneShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:Gene ;
    rdfs:label "Gene shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:expressed_in ;
        sh:name "expressed in" ;
        sh:class biolink:MaterialSample ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Gene expressed_in must point to a Sample." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:participates_in ;
        sh:name "participates in" ;
        sh:class biolink:Pathway ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Gene participates_in must point to a Pathway." ;
    ] .

shape:IndividualShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Individual ;
    rdfs:label "Individual shape" ;
    rdfs:comment "Also validates Case and Control, which are subclasses of Individual." ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_sample ;
        sh:name "has sample" ;
        sh:class biolink:MaterialSample ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Individual has_sample must point to a Sample." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_diagnosis ;
        sh:name "has diagnosis" ;
        sh:class biolink:Disease ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Individual has_diagnosis must point to a DiseaseLabel." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:staged_at ;
        sh:name "staged at" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:DiseaseStage ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Individual staged_at must point to a DiseaseStage." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_status ;
        sh:name "has status" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:DiseaseStatus ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Individual has_status must point to a DiseaseStatus." ;
    ] .

shape:OrganShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Organ ;
    rdfs:label "Organ shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_subregion ;
        sh:name "has sub-region" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:OrganSubregion ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Organ has_subregion must point to an OrganSubregion." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:contains_tissue ;
        sh:name "contains tissue" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:Tissue ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Organ contains_tissue must point to a Tissue." ;
    ] .

shape:OrganSubregionShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:OrganSubregion ;
    rdfs:label "Organ sub-region shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:contains_tissue ;
        sh:name "contains tissue" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:Tissue ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "OrganSubregion contains_tissue must point to a Tissue." ;
    ] .

shape:SampleShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:MaterialSample ;
    rdfs:label "Sample shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:source_tissue ;
        sh:name "source tissue" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:Tissue ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Sample source_tissue must point to a Tissue." ;
    ] ;
    # No sh:maxCount. The source model records this connection 1:1, but that bound
    # does not survive contact with the data: a sample can carry more than one
    # pathology statement, and they are not even the same kind of statement -- a
    # graded score (CERAD) and a qualitative finding (neurofibrillary tangles) can
    # both be true of one sample. Capping at 1 forced a choice between them. See
    # ISSUE-4 below, and B1c on whether a graded score is a node at all.
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_pathology ;
        sh:name "has pathology" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:Pathology ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Sample has_pathology must point to a Pathology." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_altered_activity_in ;
        sh:name "has altered activity in" ;
        sh:class biolink:Pathway ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Sample has_altered_activity_in must point to a Pathway." ;
    ] .


###  Systems Biology  ##########################################

# A contrast is the subject of a differential-expression result, which is why
# sagebrain:de_associated_with is domained here and not on Sample: DE is a
# property of a case-versus-control comparison, not of one specimen. The
# per-sample claim is has_altered_activity_in on SampleShape above.
#
# Both edges point at a Pathway, so the two sh:class constraints look alike. They
# are not redundant: the ontology can only state each property's range as the
# union over its uses, and it is only the per-source-shape sh:class that forbids
# a contrast from reaching a DiseaseStage. See tests/violating.ttl defects (a)
# and (g), which plant exactly that error on each of the two subjects.
shape:PathwayActivityContrastShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:PathwayActivityContrast ;
    rdfs:label "Pathway activity contrast shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:de_associated_with ;
        sh:name "DE associated with" ;
        sh:class biolink:Pathway ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Contrast de_associated_with must point to a Pathway." ;
    ] .

###  Clinical  #################################################

shape:DiseaseLabelShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:Disease ;
    rdfs:label "Disease label shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:has_trial ;
        sh:name "has trial" ;
        sh:class biolink:ClinicalTrial ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "DiseaseLabel has_trial must point to a ClinicalTrial." ;
    ] .

shape:DiseaseStatusShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:DiseaseStatus ;
    rdfs:label "Disease status shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:relates_to ;
        sh:name "relates to" ;
        sh:class biolink:Disease ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A DiseaseStatus relates_to at most one DiseaseLabel (source model: many:1)." ;
    ] .

shape:DiseaseStageShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:DiseaseStage ;
    rdfs:label "Disease stage shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:relates_to ;
        sh:name "relates to" ;
        sh:class biolink:Disease ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A DiseaseStage relates_to at most one DiseaseLabel (source model: many:1)." ;
    ] .

shape:PathologyShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Pathology ;
    rdfs:label "Pathology shape" ;
    rdfs:comment """No sh:maxCount on relates_to here: the source model records this row as
1:many, unlike the many:1 recorded for DiseaseStage and DiseaseStatus on the same property.
See issue note ISSUE-2 in the inverse-cardinality section.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:relates_to ;
        sh:name "relates to" ;
        sh:class biolink:Disease ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Pathology relates_to must point to a DiseaseLabel." ;
    ] .

shape:CaseShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Case ;
    rdfs:label "Case shape" ;
    rdfs:comment "Enforces the owl:disjointWith declared between Case and Control. OWL disjointness only yields an inconsistency under a reasoner; this makes it a validation error." ;

    sh:not [ sh:class sagebrain:Control ] ;
    sh:message "An Individual cannot be both a Case and a Control." .

shape:ControlShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Control ;
    rdfs:label "Control shape" ;

    sh:not [ sh:class sagebrain:Case ] ;
    sh:message "An Individual cannot be both a Control and a Case." .

###  Systems Biology  ##########################################

shape:PathwayShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:Pathway ;
    rdfs:label "Pathway shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:belongs_to ;
        sh:name "belongs to" ;
        sh:class sagebrain:Biodomain ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A Pathway belongs_to at most one Biodomain (source model: many:1)." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:associated_with ;
        sh:name "associated with" ;
        sh:class biolink:Disease ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Pathway associated_with must point to a DiseaseLabel." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:related_pathway ;
        sh:name "related pathway" ;
        sh:class biolink:Pathway ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "Pathway related_pathway must point to another Pathway." ;
    ] .

###  Translational  ############################################

shape:DrugCompoundShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:Drug ;
    rdfs:label "Drug compound shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:targets ;
        sh:name "targets" ;
        sh:class biolink:Gene ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "DrugCompound targets must point to a Gene." ;
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:used_to_treat ;
        sh:name "used to treat" ;
        sh:class biolink:Disease ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:message "DrugCompound used_to_treat must point to a DiseaseLabel." ;
    ] .

shape:ClinicalTrialShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:ClinicalTrial ;
    rdfs:label "Clinical trial shape" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path sagebrain:tests ;
        sh:name "tests" ;
        sh:class biolink:Drug ;
        sh:nodeKind sh:IRI ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A ClinicalTrial tests at most one DrugCompound (source model: many:1)." ;
    ] .


#################################################################
#################################################################
##
##  3. INVERSE CARDINALITY -- DEACTIVATED
##
##  The source model states cardinality as "From:To". Read strictly, the
##  "1" on the From side of a 1:many row is a constraint on the INVERSE
##  direction: at most one source per target. Compiling that mechanically
##  produces false constraints, because the model mixes two kinds of node
##  without distinguishing them:
##
##    - instance-level nodes, one per real-world thing (Individual, Sample)
##    - vocabulary-level nodes, shared across many instances
##      (DiseaseLabel "one node per disease", DiseaseStatus/DiseaseStage/
##       Pathology "multiple nodes, disease-specific", Tissue, Pathway)
##
##  An inverse cardinality of 1 is meaningful between two instance-level
##  nodes and generally false when the target is a shared vocabulary node.
##  Until the model distinguishes these, each shape below is authored with
##  sh:deactivated true and an explanation. Flip the flag once the intended
##  reading is confirmed; two cases that ARE defensible today are already
##  active and appear at the end of this section.
##
#################################################################
#################################################################

shape:InverseHasDiagnosisShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:Disease ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of has_diagnosis (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment """ISSUE-1. Strict reading of Individual --has_diagnosis--> DiseaseLabel [1:many]
is "at most one Individual per DiseaseLabel", which contradicts the class description
"one node per disease (e.g. Alzheimer's Disease, ALS)" -- every diagnosed individual points
at the same shared node. The same defect applies to has_status and staged_at below.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:has_diagnosis ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one Individual may have this DiseaseLabel as a diagnosis." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseHasStatusShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:DiseaseStatus ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of has_status (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment "ISSUE-1. DiseaseStatus is a shared vocabulary node (\"multiple nodes, disease-specific\"), so at most one Individual per status is false." ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:has_status ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one Individual may have this DiseaseStatus." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseStagedAtShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:DiseaseStage ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of staged_at (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment "ISSUE-1. DiseaseStage is a shared vocabulary node, so at most one Individual per stage is false." ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:staged_at ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one Individual may be staged at this DiseaseStage." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseRelatesToShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:Disease ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of relates_to from Pathology (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment """ISSUE-2. Pathology --relates_to--> DiseaseLabel is recorded 1:many while
DiseaseStage --relates_to--> DiseaseLabel and DiseaseStatus --relates_to--> DiseaseLabel are
recorded many:1, all on the same property. Those readings are mutually exclusive: an inverse
maxCount of 1 on DiseaseLabel would forbid the many-to-one fan-in the other two rows require.
This is not merely unconfirmed, it is inconsistent as recorded.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:relates_to ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one node may relate to this DiseaseLabel." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseSourceTissueShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Tissue ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of source_tissue (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment """ISSUE-3. Sample --source_tissue--> Tissue is recorded 1:many, but the row's
own note reads "One tissue is the source of many samples" -- the opposite orientation. Under the
recorded value each Tissue would admit at most one Sample; under the note each Sample has at
most one source Tissue, i.e. many:1 with a forward maxCount instead.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:source_tissue ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one Sample may draw from this Tissue." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseHasPathologyShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Pathology ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of has_pathology (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment """ISSUE-4. Sample --has_pathology--> Pathology is recorded 1:1, and neither
half of that survives.

The inverse half never did: Pathology is described as a classification node with "multiple
nodes, disease-specific", so many Samples are expected to share one pathology classification.
The row is probably many:1, and this shape stays deactivated.

The forward half looked defensible and was active, until writing examples/AD-cohort.ttl
showed otherwise -- a sample can carry a graded score (CERAD) and a qualitative finding
(neurofibrillary tangles) at once, which are different kinds of statement rather than
competing values. That cap is now removed from shape:SampleShape, so the connection is
unbounded in both directions.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:has_pathology ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one Sample may carry this Pathology." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseContainsTissueShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:Tissue ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of contains_tissue (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment """ISSUE-5. contains_tissue is asserted from both Organ and OrganSubregion,
each recorded 1:many. A single inverse maxCount of 1 on Tissue would forbid a Tissue being
contained by both an Organ and a sub-region of that organ -- yet the OrganSubregion row's note,
"A sub-region may also be the source of a tissue", suggests exactly that is intended. Splitting
into two qualified shapes needs the intended containment semantics settled first.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:contains_tissue ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one Organ or OrganSubregion may contain this Tissue." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseHasTrialShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:ClinicalTrial ;
    sh:deactivated true ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of has_trial (deactivated)" ;
    rdfs:comment """ISSUE-6. DiseaseLabel --has_trial--> ClinicalTrial is recorded 1:many, so
each trial would belong to at most one disease. Plausible, but nothing in the source model
corroborates it and basket or platform trials span indications. Left deactivated pending
confirmation rather than assumed.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:has_trial ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "At most one DiseaseLabel may claim this ClinicalTrial." ;
    ] .

###  Active inverse constraints  ################################
##
##  Enabled because the source model's own Description or Notes
##  independently corroborate the orientation, and both endpoints are
##  instance-level or both are vocabulary-level.

shape:InverseHasSampleShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass biolink:MaterialSample ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of has_sample" ;
    rdfs:comment """Active. Corroborated twice in the source model: the Sample description
"Many samples per individual" and the row note "One individual contributes many samples".
Both endpoints are instance-level nodes, so the constraint is meaningful.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:has_sample ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A Sample may belong to at most one Individual." ;
    ] .

shape:InverseHasSubregionShape
    a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass sagebrain:OrganSubregion ;
    rdfs:label "Inverse of has_subregion" ;
    rdfs:comment """Active. Corroborated by the OrganSubregion description, "a distinct
anatomical sub-region within an organ", and its note, "Component of Organ". Both endpoints are
vocabulary-level anatomical concepts standing in a part-of hierarchy.""" ;

    sh:property [
        sh:path [ sh:inversePath sagebrain:has_subregion ] ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "An OrganSubregion may be a sub-region of at most one Organ." ;
    ] .


#################################################################
#################################################################
##
##  4. EDGE WEIGHTS -- NOT YET CONSTRAINABLE
##
##  Three connections are marked weighted in the source model:
##    Gene expressed_in Sample; Individual has_status DiseaseStatus;
##    Pathway associated_with DiseaseLabel.
##
##  The sample-to-pathway case USED TO BE the fourth, and is now resolved rather
##  than pending: sagebrain:has_altered_activity_in took option (b) below. The
##  score, its p-values and the direction live on a reified biolink:StudyResult,
##  and the plain edge carries no number -- so the property is weighted false and
##  drops out of this list. The reified node is minted by the ingest
##  (sbkg:PathwayScoringAnalysis and friends) rather than by this ontology, which
##  is why option (b)'s "adds a class the source model does not have" caveat did
##  not bite here.
##
##  A weight is a property OF an edge, which plain RDF triples cannot carry.
##  Validating weights requires first choosing how they are materialised:
##
##    (a) not in RDF at all -- weights live only in the property-graph load,
##        and this ontology stays a schema-level description. No shapes needed.
##    (b) reification -- introduce an association class carrying source,
##        target and an xsd:decimal weight. Fully validatable with SHACL today,
##        but it adds a class to the ontology that the source model does not have.
##    (c) RDF-star / RDF 1.2 quoted triples -- closest to the property-graph
##        model, but SHACL support across tooling is still thin.
##    (d) don't represent the number -- represent its interpretation, as
##        directional predicates (de_over_* / de_under_*) derived by
##        thresholding whatever score the method produced. Constrainable with
##        ordinary sh:class shapes, so this is the one option that needs no new
##        machinery here; it trades magnitude away and moves the threshold into
##        the pipeline. See B11 for the full argument.
##
##  No shapes are emitted here on purpose: each option implies a different
##  instance representation, and picking one is a modelling decision rather
##  than a validation detail. The sagebrain:weighted flag on each object
##  property records which edges would be in scope.
##
#################################################################
#################################################################
